<!--Post: 4419--><!--Time: 1106344233-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4419"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Jrl<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Jrl<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, January 21, 2005 - 1:50 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Read &#34;On Desire&#34; and tell me if it doesn&#39;t sound like he took bits and pieces of it to write &#34;Anna&#34;. <BR> <BR><a href="http://64.233.167.104/search?q=cache:j2jLNlTWCAsJ:classiclit.about.com/library/bl-etexts/abehn/bl-abehn-ondesire.htm%2Bpoetry%2BAND%2Bpropension&amp;hl=en" target=_top>http://64.233.167.104/search?q=cache:j2jLNlTWCAsJ: classiclit.about.com/library/bl-etexts/abehn/bl-ab ehn-ondesire.htm&#43;poetry&#43;AND&#43;propension&amp;hl=en</a><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4419-->
<!--Post: 4467--><!--Time: 1106394129-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4467"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 22, 2005 - 3:42 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Hi Jrl, Good question! I&#39;m an intelligent person but the only poems I can come close to appreciating are prose poems. The lyrical stuff baffles me. Anyway, they are some well-read poetry <BR>people on this board and you should hear from one of them at some point today.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4467-->
<!--Post: 4470--><!--Time: 1106397326-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4470"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 22, 2005 - 4:35 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Jrl, here is a brief poem I and two others agreed was the sort of thing BTK would write if he had the talent. The problem is that it&#39;s extremely difficult to figure it out. I&#39;d appreciate your thoughts very much. Thanks! <BR> <BR> <BR><a href="http://www.cityintherain.com/poems/vitalx.html" target=_top>http://www.cityintherain.com/poems/vitalx.html</a><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4470-->
<!--Post: 4494--><!--Time: 1106432662-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4494"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Pax56<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Pax56<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 22, 2005 - 2:24 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Hi Jrl - thanks for the poem.  I can&#39;t prove it, but I think you just hit paydirt with that one.  Proof at least to me of the net theory that &#34;Everybody&#34; is smarter than &#34;Anybody&#34;.  The fact that several key elements of the &#34;Anna&#34; poem are found in this one, together with the title being a humorous reference to his topic, cinches it for me.  Alas, it also means that Tom&#39;s new-found wisdom &#40;his entry page comments, which he may have since taken down&#41; that BTK is perhaps smarter than he thought is well-founded, in my opinion.  That darn word &#34;propension&#34; was &#40;in my mind now&#41; neither a linguistic error, a Hispanic influence, nor a stupid plagiarism.  It was a deliberate, mocking clue leading to this poem, and left about 15 years ago, to boot. <BR> <BR>It means that BTK didn&#39;t just do a cheap hatchet job, or engage in rank plagiarism, but deliberately chose this poem as a meaningful part of his framework, all with his dark sense of humor.  As to your question of whether he was a fan or not:  I would say he chose it for the title and subject, not for any favoritism.  He either knew of it, or how to find it, or was familiar with it.  He could have been a fan, certainly. <BR> <BR>George - a few minutes ago, I would have discounted completely the idea that BTK could have written something like the poem you cite, because it&#39;s just too deep.  Now - well, I&#39;m not so sure.  There is still &#40;in my opinion&#41; a lot more depth to the second poem than to BTK&#39;s, but I now doubt that he was trying to &#34;pass the ball as deep as he could&#34;, so to speak, with the Anna poem.  Still, I remain in agreement with Steve on the poetry thread that BTK isn&#39;t a poetic genius - perhaps just a poetic strangler, as he suggested all along.  I agree with your assessment that the &#34;Motive&#34; poem is something that BTK would write if he could.  Although I smell a little too much gunpowder in it for BTK&#39;s &#34;everyday&#34; work, I would no longer be surprised if his mind wanders in new and different directions.  One thing, though, is that &#34;Motive&#34; requires a bit too much empathy on the part of the writer - more than I think BTK has.  I think he depersonalizes others with either dark humor about their suffering, ignorance of their suffering, and even ignorance of their status as thinking beings.  I suspect that attributing &#34;motive&#34; to others - or to himself beyond his admitted sexual desires - is beyond him, or is something so painful to him that he chooses almost instinctively to ignore it. <BR> <BR>All in all, I think the poetry angle is productive.  I think that after BTK is identified, there will be a cottage industry devoted to both his known and possible poetry, be it good or bad.  However, it would be nicer to find any public stash beforehand, to help assure that the joyous event of his capture occurs within his lifetime.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4494-->
<!--Post: 4500--><!--Time: 1106437201-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4500"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Jrl<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Jrl<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 22, 2005 - 3:40 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Hello Pax.  You are seeing within the poem the same things I saw.  As you said, I can&#39;t prove it, but it is a possibility.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4500-->
<!--Post: 4504--><!--Time: 1106443554-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4504"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Skov<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Skov<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 22, 2005 - 5:25 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Very interesting development IMO.  <BR>Here is som more poems , and info about the poet APHRA BEHN. <BR>It is very interesting reading, but because enlish is not my first languish I would rather that somone else read through AB´s other poems. <BR> <BR><a href="http://www.google.dk/search?q=cache:ooo7njMrsooJ:www.poetry-archive.com/b/behn_aphra.html%2B%22by%2BAphra%2BBehn%22%2B%2B%22On%2BDesire%22&amp;hl=en&amp;lr=lang_en" target=_top>http://www.google.dk/search?q=cache:ooo7njMrsooJ:w ww.poetry-archive.com/b/behn_aphra.html&#43;%22by&#43;Aphr a&#43;Behn%22&#43;%2B%22On&#43;Desire%22&amp;hl=en&amp;lr=lang_en</a><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4504-->
<!--Post: 4505--><!--Time: 1106447409-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4505"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Flytodeb<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Flytodeb<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 22, 2005 - 6:30 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Jrl, thanks for the link to &#34;On Desire.&#34; The word propension really jumped out, didn&#39;t it? Great find! George, Pax, and Skov, thanks for your messages.  <BR> <BR>Perhaps the &#34;Oh Anna&#34; poem seems so strange to us because we know it was written by a serial killer who has been labeled antisocial. His poem is a weak attempt to try and convince us that he is a social being. JMO <BR>-Deb  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4505-->
<!--Post: 4506--><!--Time: 1106449011-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4506"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Jrl<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Jrl<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 22, 2005 - 6:56 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Hey fly.  What also jumped out was her waiting for desire to appear, but finding it not and the parts about browers and grass and of course, the propension. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4506-->
<!--Post: 4508--><!--Time: 1106449658-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4508"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 22, 2005 - 7:07 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->This is the best way a website can be of use; excellent contributions by all. Quickly, the Wallace Stevns poem, &#34;The Motive For Metaphor,&#34; can never really be interpreted accurately. Stevens wrote extensively about &#34;motive&#34; in poetry and points out numerous titles of his own poems to prove the point. I have yet to find one person who can say with confidence that they understand &#34;The Motive For Metaphor.&#34; Stevens was <BR>a firm believer in poem construction and he wrote countless papers on &#34;motive&#34; but only in a poetic sense. The Google searches I did show a Stevens who was nearly obsessed with poetic motive. Poetic motive was his favorite aspect of poetic composition. I only found this earlier today. <BR> <BR>Great eye, Jrl! What you&#39;ve discovered is remarkable and studious. <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4508-->
<!--Post: 4513--><!--Time: 1106455417-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4513"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Jrl<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Jrl<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 22, 2005 - 8:43 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Thanks George.  Although I&#39;m not quite sure it&#39;s related, it was interesting anyway.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4513-->
</table></td></tr></table>
